WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.445
Linux AI GPU reliability depends on the full stack: hardware, firmware, kernel modules,

00:00:05.445 --> 00:00:09.146
user-space libraries, display or compute components, containers, model runtimes,

00:00:09.146 --> 00:00:09.969
and applications.

00:00:09.969 --> 00:00:13.669
A GPU being physically present is only the beginning.

00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:18.604
A usable acceleration stack must be compatible and validated from the point

00:00:18.604 --> 00:00:20.660
where the workload actually runs.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.360
Strong practice uses planned versions, documented configurations, compatibility checks,

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.239
validation tests, monitoring, backups, and controlled updates.

00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:31.762
Driver versions, kernels, CUDA or ROCm components, container images, Python environments,

00:00:31.762 --> 00:00:36.696
and inference runtimes should be treated as related parts of one system.

00:00:36.696 --> 00:00:39.163
Random version mixing makes troubleshooting harder.

00:00:39.163 --> 00:00:41.630
Validation should prove more than detection.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:46.565
Check driver load status, management tools, GPU visibility inside containers or runtimes,

00:00:46.565 --> 00:00:50.265
simple inference smoke tests, performance behavior, utilization, memory, temperature,

00:00:50.265 --> 00:00:51.088
and throttling.

00:00:51.088 --> 00:00:56.844
Use those checks to separate driver issues from model size, context length, runtime settings,

00:00:56.844 --> 00:00:58.900
permission problems, and container configuration.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.012
The goal is not simply to install the newest driver.

00:01:03.012 --> 00:01:05.891
The goal is to build a stable,

00:01:05.891 --> 00:01:09.180
repeatable acceleration stack that supports the AI workload.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.526
A well-managed Linux GPU system is documented, recoverable, monitored, and updated with intent.

00:01:14.526 --> 00:01:19.460
That is what makes local AI, inference servers, and development systems dependable.
